diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the FRR OSPF daemon (ospfd) on multiple core routers. Mechanism: a near-simultaneous restart of ospfd reset all OSPF adjacencies, causing a burst of neighbor state changes, SPF runs, and transient route churn across the campus area, which matches the brief internal path drops reported.

Localization: All core routers in Area 0 show the same pattern. On router7, router8, router10, router11, router16, and router17, ospfd process uptimes are ~260 seconds (ps etimes), and “show ip ospf neighbor/detail” shows “Most recent state change” ~2–3 minutes ago with identical “6 state changes” across neighbors. Current adjacencies are Full on all links (e.g., router7 neighbors on 192.168.10.0/24, 10.0.0.0/24, 172.30.1.0/24, 172.25.1.0/24 are stable), and OSPF interface timers/costs match. This rules out a single unstable adjacency (router7↔router8 or an mswitch uplink).

Root cause: FRR OSPF services were restarted almost simultaneously on the campus routers, not a link-layer instability.

Corrective fix: Stop any automation or maintenance job that restarts FRR en masse. If daemons must be restarted, stagger them to avoid area-wide churn. Additionally, enable OSPF Graceful Restart/NSF on all nodes (and helper on neighbors) to preserve forwarding during control-plane restarts, and enable FRR logging to capture future events for quick correlation. Monitoring now shows the area is stable post-restart.
=== END ===